WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.320
We are looking at a mechanical metronome swinging with violent precision.

2
00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.720
It represents the absolute physical limit of mobile UI performance.

3
00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:11.760
To hit a flawless 120 frames per second,

4
00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:16.960
your application has exactly 8 milliseconds to calculate and render a single frame.

5
00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:21.840
This architectural diagram shows how traditional React Native handles that deadline.

6
00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:25.840
On the left is the main JavaScript thread, where your AppLogic lives.

7
00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:29.520
On the right is the Native UI thread, which actually draws the pixels.

8
00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:36.400
To animate anything, the JS thread has to send a serialized message across an asynchronous bridge to the UI thread.

9
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:40.720
When standard application logic, like fetching data or processing state,

10
00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:45.360
clogs up that JS thread. Those asynchronous animation messages get delayed.

11
00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:48.960
The window to render the frame closes before the instructions even arrive,

12
00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.360
resulting in dropped frames and a stuttering interface.

13
00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:56.080
Re-enabited three abandons this declarative handoff model entirely.

14
00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:58.960
Instead of sending instructions across a bridge,

15
00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:04.000
re-animated achieves perfect frame rates by running your JavaScript imperative logic

16
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.800
directly on the UI thread itself.

17
00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:11.760
Building fluid mobile applications requires mastering this dual thread system,

18
00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:16.480
stripping animation logic completely away from standard application state.

19
00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:20.160
This architectural separation begins during the build process,

20
00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:23.200
powered by a specific Babel compiler plugin.

21
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:27.920
Developers use the Worklet string directive at the top of a JavaScript function.

22
00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:33.200
This explicitly marks that specific block of code for execution on the UI thread.

23
00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:37.760
The compiler locates the function, extracts its source and closure variables,

24
00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:40.720
and serializes them into a packaged node.

25
00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:45.280
At runtime, this bundle installs into a secondary Hermes virtual machine.

26
00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:48.800
This VM operates exclusively on the Native UI thread,

27
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:51.920
physically separated from the main JavaScript runtime.

28
00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:57.280
While standalone helper functions require you to manually write the Worklet directive,

29
00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:00.080
functions passed in line to re-animated hooks,

30
00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:02.960
like animated styles or gesture callbacks,

31
00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:06.160
are automatically identified and processed by the compiler.

32
00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:08.640
Through built-time compilation,

33
00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:12.240
re-animated takes a single unified JavaScript code base

34
00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:16.400
and physically divides it across two distinct execution environments.

35
00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:19.600
When a user swipes or drags an element on the screen,

36
00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:22.880
that gesture event originates natively on the UI thread.

37
00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:27.680
That native interaction instantly triggers the invocation of our compiled Worklet.

38
00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:30.400
This diagram outlines the communication layer.

39
00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.400
Instead of an asynchronous bridge,

40
00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.920
re-animated uses JSI, the JavaScript interface,

41
00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:40.000
to directly connect the native UI to our secondary Hermes VM.

42
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:45.600
The Worklet function is invoked and processes the gesture math entirely within that UI thread VM.

43
00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:49.120
Because of JSI, this execution happens synchronously.

44
00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:53.040
It blocks the UI thread for only fractions of a millisecond,

45
00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.280
finishing well within the current frame,

46
00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:59.120
and requires zero communication with the main React JavaScript thread.

47
00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:03.440
By isolating the animation calculations over JSI,

48
00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:08.080
re-animated prevents standard application logic from interfering with the frame rate,

49
00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:10.480
rendering UI stutters virtually impossible.

50
00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:14.720
To share state between these two isolated Hermes runtimes,

51
00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:18.080
we use a specific data primitive called use-shared value.

52
00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:24.400
In this thread geometry, when the UI thread reads or writes to a shared value inside a Worklet,

53
00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:27.360
the operation is immediate and synchronous on the same frame.

54
00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:31.600
However, when the JavaScript thread updates that same shared value,

55
00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:34.560
the write is a synchronous arriving before the next frame.

56
00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:39.200
This creates a pitfall, writing to a shared value on the JS thread,

57
00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:42.560
then reading it immediately returns a stale value.

58
00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:46.880
Furthermore, reading that .value forces the main JS thread

59
00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:51.120
to completely freeze, halting execution until the UI thread

60
00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:53.200
delivers the payload across the boundary.

61
00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:58.000
Despite these strict access rules, the memory bridge is versatile,

62
00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:02.800
supporting everything from raw numbers and hex colors to complex objects and arrays.

63
00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:07.040
Treating these shared values like standard React state

64
00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:09.280
ignores their physical location and memory.

65
00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:13.840
Relying on synchronous assumptions across asynchronous boundaries

66
00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:16.480
leads directly to race conditions and thread locks.

67
00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:20.240
To map these shared values to visual properties,

68
00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:22.160
we use use animated style.

69
00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:26.720
This hook computes a style object directly on the UI thread,

70
00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:30.720
ensuring styles update frame by frame without JS thread intervention.

71
00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.760
Only dynamic properties belong here.

72
00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:36.400
Static properties, like borders or padding,

73
00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:38.880
should remain in standard React style sheets,

74
00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:42.000
removing the overhead of evaluating them on every single frame.

75
00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:46.560
When a complex animation requires pure mathematical transformations

76
00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:48.400
based on other shared values,

77
00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:51.200
we compute them using used derived value,

78
00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:54.080
which creates a read-only node in the reactive graph.

79
00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:57.840
This flow chart illustrates a critical API rule,

80
00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:02.960
never write to a shared value from within the callback actively reading from it.

81
00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:06.800
Mutating state here creates an infinite feedback loop.

82
00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:09.280
The callback continuously triggers itself,

83
00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:12.400
rapidly accelerating until the thread completely crashes.

84
00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:17.760
Reanimated's reactive graph is built as a strict one-way data pipeline.

85
00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:20.160
To remain stable, your reactive callbacks

86
00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:23.360
must operate strictly as pure read-only functions.

87
00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:28.960
A common source of developer friction occurs when variables seem to mysteriously freeze,

88
00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:31.440
failing to update inside a running workload.

89
00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:32.960
This split view explains why.

90
00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:36.480
Remember the build time serialization process.

91
00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:41.600
A workload's closure references are permanently locked at the exact moment the app compiles.

92
00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:44.800
When standard React state changes on the main JS thread,

93
00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:49.040
there is no physical mechanism to transport that new value into the locked closure.

94
00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:55.440
To bypass this limitation, you must use shared values as a designated live data channel,

95
00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:58.320
keeping the UI thread supplied with current information.

96
00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:03.360
When the workload finishes its task and needs to inform the rest of the application,

97
00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:05.360
we use the run on JS function.

98
00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:08.480
This provides an asynchronous escape hatch,

99
00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:12.960
letting the UI thread dispatch completion callbacks back to the main react event loop.

100
00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:17.520
Conversely, run on UI serves as the inverse conduit,

101
00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:22.880
giving the main JavaScript thread the ability to manually schedule worklet executions on the UI thread.

102
00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:29.120
These manual threading functions are the required tags for operating isolated virtual machines,

103
00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:32.640
maintaining strict execution boundaries guarantees performance,

104
00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:35.360
but it demands precise explicit communication.

105
00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:39.520
We are observing a continuous high velocity render.

106
00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:44.320
This visual represents the fluid momentum achievable when UI calculations execute

107
00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:50.080
without system hesitation. Consider a scroll event mapped to use animated scroll handler.

108
00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:52.800
The native scroll triggers the UI worklet,

109
00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:57.600
which updates a shared value, which instantly recalculates an animated style.

110
00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:01.120
The visual updates perfectly, frame by frame,

111
00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:04.160
while the main JavaScript thread remains completely asleep.

112
00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:10.640
To navigate this environment, developers rely on the global.worklet boolean as a definitive

113
00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:16.480
runtime diagnostic tool. Evaluating this variable provides a hard environmental assertion,

114
00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:22.400
proving exactly which of the two isolated Hermes runtimes is currently executing a given block of code.

115
00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:28.560
Adopting reanimated 3 requires abandoning the mindset of treating mobile animations as simple,

116
00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:34.560
declarative configuration files. Achieving flawless execution under physical time constraints means

117
00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:39.280
treating animations as isolated concurrent software running on the UI thread.

118
00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:45.280
It demands absolute mastery over thread boundaries, memory synchronization and the dual VM architecture.

